1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:01,440
Your card just said approved.

2
00:00:01,920 --> 00:00:04,100
The coffee shop hasn't been
paid, and it won't be today

3
00:00:05,213 --> 00:00:06,413
Approved is a message.

4
00:00:06,833 --> 00:00:10,113
The money moves later through two more
systems most people never hear about

5
00:00:11,200 --> 00:00:14,400
By the end, you'll know where every
line in your bank app comes from:

6
00:00:14,780 --> 00:00:17,400
pending, posted, and
the one that disappears

7
00:00:18,487 --> 00:00:19,667
Start with the tap.

8
00:00:19,667 --> 00:00:24,033
Your card, or your phone, hands the
terminal a card number and a one-time

9
00:00:24,033 --> 00:00:24,387
code.

10
00:00:25,587 --> 00:00:27,507
That code exists for this purchase only.

11
00:00:28,147 --> 00:00:31,167
Copy it and replay it tomorrow
and the bank rejects it

12
00:00:32,353 --> 00:00:34,473
That code is called a cryptogram.

13
00:00:35,353 --> 00:00:39,213
The chip or your phone computes it
from a secret key that never leaves the

14
00:00:39,233 --> 00:00:41,633
device, plus the amount
and a running counter.

15
00:00:42,433 --> 00:00:44,853
Your bank holds the matching
key so it can check the math

16
00:00:46,033 --> 00:00:49,293
Pay with your phone and the terminal
never sees your real card number.

17
00:00:49,893 --> 00:00:53,573
It gets a token, a stand-in number
that only works from that device

18
00:00:54,700 --> 00:00:58,400
If a shop's system gets hacked, a
stolen token is useless anywhere else

19
00:00:59,500 --> 00:01:01,600
The terminal passes
everything to the shop's bank,

20
00:01:01,980 --> 00:01:02,820
called the acquirer.

21
00:01:03,459 --> 00:01:04,760
The acquirer doesn't know you.

22
00:01:05,300 --> 00:01:06,800
It only knows where to send the question

23
00:01:07,893 --> 00:01:11,053
It sends it to the card
network, Visa or Mastercard.

24
00:01:11,953 --> 00:01:14,893
The network reads the first six to
eight digits of the card number,

25
00:01:15,433 --> 00:01:18,113
finds the bank that issued
it, and forwards the message

26
00:01:19,267 --> 00:01:21,627
The network holds none of your
money and lends you nothing.

27
00:01:22,107 --> 00:01:24,847
It carries messages and
charges a small fee on each one

28
00:01:26,767 --> 00:01:28,207
Now open the message itself.

29
00:01:29,067 --> 00:01:32,847
Card networks use a
format called ISO 8583,

30
00:01:33,427 --> 00:01:35,067
first defined in 1987

31
00:01:36,147 --> 00:01:38,427
The first four digits say
what kind of message it is.

32
00:01:38,987 --> 00:01:42,607
Zero one zero zero means,
"May I charge this card?"

33
00:01:43,733 --> 00:01:45,924
Then come numbered fields.

34
00:01:45,924 --> 00:01:48,196
Field 2 is the card number.

35
00:01:48,196 --> 00:01:50,062
Field 4 is the amount.

36
00:01:50,062 --> 00:01:52,172
Field 49 is the currency.

37
00:01:53,353 --> 00:01:56,613
Your bank, the issuer, now has
well under a second to decide

38
00:01:57,713 --> 00:02:01,213
It checks that the card is real,
that the one-time code matches,

39
00:02:01,813 --> 00:02:05,633
that the purchase doesn't look like
fraud, and that you have the money or

40
00:02:05,672 --> 00:02:06,113
the credit

41
00:02:07,233 --> 00:02:09,853
The fraud check compares this
purchase with your history,

42
00:02:10,333 --> 00:02:13,593
where you usually shop, at
what time, and for how much

43
00:02:14,680 --> 00:02:17,140
Many banks turn all of
that into a risk score.

44
00:02:18,180 --> 00:02:19,860
A low score is approved at once.

45
00:02:20,560 --> 00:02:23,320
A middle score might send you
a text asking if it was you.

46
00:02:24,260 --> 00:02:25,620
A high score is declined.

47
00:02:26,520 --> 00:02:29,240
That's why a trip abroad
can suddenly stop your card

48
00:02:30,347 --> 00:02:34,653
If everything passes, it replies
with message zero one one zero,

49
00:02:34,653 --> 00:02:36,707
and field 39 set to two zeros.

50
00:02:36,707 --> 00:02:38,427
Two zeros means approved.

51
00:02:39,533 --> 00:02:42,273
Field 38 carries a
six-character approval code.

52
00:02:42,673 --> 00:02:43,433
The shop keeps it.

53
00:02:43,993 --> 00:02:45,913
For now, it's the only thing the shop has

54
00:02:47,053 --> 00:02:51,159
When the answer is no, field
39 carries a different code.

55
00:02:51,159 --> 00:02:53,708
Fifty-one means insufficient funds.

56
00:02:53,708 --> 00:02:58,451
Zero five means do not honor: the
bank says no without saying why.

57
00:02:59,567 --> 00:03:01,707
The terminal usually
shows one word for both:

58
00:03:02,267 --> 00:03:02,727
declined.

59
00:03:04,027 --> 00:03:07,727
The real reason stays inside the
message, which is why the cashier can't

60
00:03:07,787 --> 00:03:08,767
tell you what went wrong

61
00:03:10,580 --> 00:03:12,740
On a yes, that reply is the beep you hear.

62
00:03:13,360 --> 00:03:15,260
Now count the money that
moved between banks.

63
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:16,300
Zero

64
00:03:17,447 --> 00:03:19,627
Instead, your bank put
a hold on five dollars.

65
00:03:20,207 --> 00:03:21,947
Your available balance went down by five.

66
00:03:22,467 --> 00:03:23,987
Your actual balance stayed the same

67
00:03:25,187 --> 00:03:27,127
That hold is the pending line in your app.

68
00:03:27,747 --> 00:03:31,347
In most banking apps, pending and
posted come from two different records,

69
00:03:31,707 --> 00:03:34,287
which is why the name or the
amount can change between them

70
00:03:35,433 --> 00:03:39,293
Gas pumps and hotels often hold more
than you'll spend because they don't

71
00:03:39,333 --> 00:03:40,453
know the final amount yet

72
00:03:41,580 --> 00:03:44,080
A hotel can also raise
the hold during your stay,

73
00:03:44,680 --> 00:03:46,760
and the final charge can
differ from the first amount.

74
00:03:47,640 --> 00:03:50,240
Card rules give hotels and
car rentals room to do that,

75
00:03:50,880 --> 00:03:52,260
and a restaurant can add your tip

76
00:03:53,367 --> 00:03:55,907
If the shop never follows
up, the hold expires.

77
00:03:56,907 --> 00:03:59,547
For a purchase like this, that
is usually a matter of days.

78
00:04:00,147 --> 00:04:01,927
That's the pending charge that disappears

79
00:04:03,800 --> 00:04:05,760
Then there's the charge
that shows up twice

80
00:04:06,867 --> 00:04:10,367
Sometimes the request reaches your
bank, but the reply gets lost on the way

81
00:04:10,407 --> 00:04:10,707
back.

82
00:04:11,707 --> 00:04:13,587
The terminal can't tell
whether you were approved,

83
00:04:13,987 --> 00:04:14,787
so it asks again

84
00:04:15,900 --> 00:04:17,440
Your bank said yes both times.

85
00:04:17,660 --> 00:04:19,000
Two holds, one coffee

86
00:04:20,147 --> 00:04:22,930
To clean it up, the
terminal sends a reversal,

87
00:04:22,930 --> 00:04:27,372
a message in the zero four hundred
family that tells your bank to drop the

88
00:04:27,372 --> 00:04:28,082
first hold.

89
00:04:28,082 --> 00:04:30,985
Until it arrives, you see
the same charge twice.

90
00:04:32,867 --> 00:04:35,747
Sometimes the bank doesn't see the
purchase at all until hours later

91
00:04:36,880 --> 00:04:40,400
On a flight with no connection, the
card and the terminal can approve the

92
00:04:40,460 --> 00:04:43,800
purchase offline within limits
set on the chip and the terminal

93
00:04:44,933 --> 00:04:48,093
The bank finds out after landing
when the airline uploads the sales.

94
00:04:49,133 --> 00:04:52,493
If the card is no good by then, the
airline can end up taking the loss

95
00:04:53,600 --> 00:04:56,480
That's one reason an airline might
cap what you can buy on board.

96
00:04:57,380 --> 00:04:59,880
A higher limit means
a bigger possible loss

97
00:05:00,967 --> 00:05:03,527
Back on the ground, the coffee
shop gets paid through clearing.

98
00:05:04,347 --> 00:05:07,807
At the end of the day, its terminal
sends every sale in one batch

99
00:05:08,953 --> 00:05:12,313
Each record carries its approval code
so the network can match it to the

100
00:05:12,353 --> 00:05:13,413
approval from this morning

101
00:05:14,553 --> 00:05:15,953
The amount doesn't have to match.

102
00:05:16,693 --> 00:05:18,753
At a restaurant, the card
is approved for the bill,

103
00:05:19,113 --> 00:05:21,333
and the tip gets added
before the batch goes out

104
00:05:22,487 --> 00:05:25,167
Pay in another currency
and there is one more step.

105
00:05:25,847 --> 00:05:30,107
The amount gets converted, and your
bank may add a foreign transaction fee,

106
00:05:30,567 --> 00:05:33,027
so your statement can differ
from your own quick math

107
00:05:34,187 --> 00:05:37,447
When the cleared record reaches your
bank, the hold turns into a real

108
00:05:37,507 --> 00:05:37,887
charge.

109
00:05:38,767 --> 00:05:41,567
Pending becomes posted,
sometimes with a different amount

110
00:05:42,680 --> 00:05:44,900
Everything so far is
the dual message path.

111
00:05:45,520 --> 00:05:48,240
One message to approve, a
second record to charge.

112
00:05:49,120 --> 00:05:51,300
Credit card purchases
typically go this way

113
00:05:52,433 --> 00:05:54,433
Some debit payments use a single message.

114
00:05:55,313 --> 00:05:57,313
In the US, when you type
your PIN at checkout,

115
00:05:57,793 --> 00:06:01,713
message zero two zero zero
approves and charges in one go

116
00:06:02,867 --> 00:06:04,667
There's no separate
clearing record to wait for,

117
00:06:04,867 --> 00:06:06,087
so the charge posts sooner.

118
00:06:06,707 --> 00:06:08,887
The shop still waits for
settlement like everyone else

119
00:06:10,820 --> 00:06:11,800
You've now been charged.

120
00:06:12,240 --> 00:06:13,660
The coffee shop still has no money.

121
00:06:14,140 --> 00:06:15,420
That's the third system's job

122
00:06:16,533 --> 00:06:19,893
The network adds up every cleared
purchase between every pair of banks,

123
00:06:20,333 --> 00:06:21,213
then nets them out.

124
00:06:22,287 --> 00:06:25,987
Without netting, every purchase would
be its own transfer between banks,

125
00:06:26,527 --> 00:06:29,687
and Visa alone handles hundreds
of millions of transactions a day

126
00:06:30,853 --> 00:06:33,473
Your bank sends one total
for millions of purchases,

127
00:06:33,993 --> 00:06:35,213
minus what other banks owe it.

128
00:06:36,093 --> 00:06:38,093
Your five dollars is a
tiny part of that number

129
00:06:39,267 --> 00:06:42,167
Those totals are settled between
banks in one shared place,

130
00:06:42,627 --> 00:06:44,067
often accounts at the central bank.

131
00:06:45,027 --> 00:06:47,967
Money moves between those accounts
once or a few times a day,

132
00:06:48,367 --> 00:06:49,367
not once per purchase

133
00:06:50,453 --> 00:06:54,193
Then the acquirer pays the coffee shop,
usually one to three business days

134
00:06:54,273 --> 00:06:54,913
after the sale

135
00:06:56,013 --> 00:06:57,533
Settlement runs on business days.

136
00:06:58,053 --> 00:07:01,213
A purchase on Friday night often
reaches the shop's account on Monday or

137
00:07:01,293 --> 00:07:01,733
Tuesday

138
00:07:02,900 --> 00:07:05,757
Fees come out before that money
lands, so the shop gets less than five

139
00:07:05,757 --> 00:07:06,119
dollars.

140
00:07:07,667 --> 00:07:10,967
The shop usually sees them as one
percentage on a monthly statement from its

141
00:07:10,987 --> 00:07:13,947
acquirer, with several
smaller fees folded inside.

142
00:07:15,747 --> 00:07:17,447
Even then, approved isn't final.

143
00:07:18,127 --> 00:07:21,127
If you dispute the charge, the money
can travel back weeks later through the

144
00:07:21,187 --> 00:07:21,867
same networks.

145
00:07:22,447 --> 00:07:23,227
That's a chargeback

146
00:07:24,333 --> 00:07:28,033
The shop gets a window to fight it
with a receipt and the six-character

147
00:07:28,093 --> 00:07:29,273
approval code from the start.

148
00:07:30,173 --> 00:07:32,433
The same code doing its last job

149
00:07:33,547 --> 00:07:35,047
A refund is a separate thing.

150
00:07:35,687 --> 00:07:39,267
The shop sends a new credit back
through the same path in the other

151
00:07:39,327 --> 00:07:41,967
direction, so it takes
its own days to arrive.

152
00:07:42,867 --> 00:07:45,147
The original charge usually
stays on your statement

153
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:49,458
So: approved is your
bank's promise to pay.

154
00:07:49,458 --> 00:07:50,687
Clearing is the bill.

155
00:07:50,687 --> 00:07:54,652
Settlement is the money, arriving a
day or more after you finished the

156
00:07:54,652 --> 00:07:55,099
coffee.

157
00:07:56,967 --> 00:07:59,827
Those fees decide how much of
your five dollars the shop keeps.

158
00:08:00,907 --> 00:08:01,527
Next video.

159
00:08:02,207 --> 00:08:05,247
On a hundred dollar purchase,
who takes a cut and how much?
